# SECURITY KNOWN GAP — a `Box`ed trait object minted inside a CLOSURE and
# returned leaks its allocation.
#
#   ASan (C, detect_leaks=1):
#     Direct leak of 32 byte(s): __gorget_box_alloc_Robot
#                                <- __Closure_0__call <- main
#     (plus a separate 3-byte thunk residual, a DIFFERENT known defect)
#
# The value twin of this program, `tests/fixtures/box_trait_closure_return.gg`,
# has been GREEN this whole time: it is wired as a stdout comparison only
# (`box_trait_closure_return` in tests/integration.rs, asserting `R2`), and a
# stdout comparison structurally cannot observe a leak. Core #13 — pick an
# instrument that can SEE the failure class; the value lane never could.
#
# DISCRIMINATED from `t0526` by ALLOCATION SOURCE: that one leaks the trait
# pack from a DISCARDED `Box.new`, where the value is never consumed. Here the
# box IS consumed — returned through the closure's call thunk and spoken
# through — and still leaks, so "discarded mint" is not the mechanism.
#
# INTENDED: prints `R2` and is ASan-clean under detect_leaks=1. The mint inside
# the closure body must be registered for drop at its birth (Core #3).
#
# RED-verified 2026-08-27 at pristine HEAD.
from std.collections import Box
trait Speaker:
    String speak(self)
struct Robot:
    String id
equip Robot with Speaker:
    String speak(self):
        return self.id
void main():
    Callable[Box[Speaker]()] f = (): Box.new(Robot("R2"))
    print(f().speak())
